                        "glosses": [
                            "Divination par le moyen d'un crible qu'on faisait tourner."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3109cc4e-feee-426f-9658-1451e98b7e9d",
                                "sense_id": "a63b867b-f54a-3e5d-977c-0324443318c5",
                                "text": "Le crible magique, dont parlent Cicéron et Lucien, eut une si grande vogue au moyen âge, qu’il devint la base de toute une science : la coscinomancie."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "96a8792d-da58-43a8-bff9-2c812088b308",
                        "source_entry_id": "13720718-b563-35c6-a0a5-f32d0a3c956b",
                        "source_sense_id": "a63b867b-f54a-3e5d-977c-0324443318c5",
                        "target_lang_word": "coscinomancy",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "coscinomancy",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "36a36588-0ba7-35ae-a1fc-526b8c50e814",
                                        "entry_id": "fbaa78f9-93e2-3f08-a9a0-21e20c0335ba",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Divination by the use of a suspended sieve sometimes from tongs or shears. The movement of the sieve when a person's name or word is spoken is interpreted."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8ee45ede-157d-4e5d-b8b2-1b0078159016",
                                                "sense_id": "36a36588-0ba7-35ae-a1fc-526b8c50e814",
                                                "text": "1603 Christopher Heydon A Defence of Ivdiciall Astrolgie\nAnd as for Hydromancie, and Choschinomancie, they could vanish as superfluous, as were evident and ridiculous even to the ignorant."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d6ddd5f9-cfaa-40ea-9e76-31c9d23ef85d",
                                                "sense_id": "36a36588-0ba7-35ae-a1fc-526b8c50e814",
                                                "text": "1660 Urquhart tr. Rabelais Gargantua & Pantagruel iii. xxv.\nBy Coscinomancy, most religiously observed of old, amidst the Ceremonies of the ancient Romans. Let us have a Sieve and Shiers, and thou shalt see Devils."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "82f2a446-479c-4226-89e5-af368753b639",
                                                "sense_id": "36a36588-0ba7-35ae-a1fc-526b8c50e814",
                                                "text": "1913 Halliday Greek Div. x.\nTo the same species of divinatory rites [i.e. those involving a swinging pendulum] belong the koskinomancy of Theokritos, familiar in England as the consultation of the sieve and shears, and the minor rites of axinomancy and sphondylomancy."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
